 S~S Consumers of metadata expect it to be in some standard form

------------------------------------------

    Specific words and phrases in <WWWWH> form can clarify the meaning of the information you write.

    A human speaker saying "<who> <what> <when> <where> <how>" expects the audience to predict
    the next word in sequence if the speaker stops at any point.  Humans build an expectation
    of what the speaker will say, based on what the speaker has said in the sequence so far.

    Popular speakers say what most listeners think they will say next; comedians say the unexpected.

    The metadata describes everything except the <why>; it can only say where <why> is described.
    Each <WWWWH> word can be resolved to a unique set of values which allows a database lookup.

        A selecter in a database is a "tuple", the unique key to select the database items.
        A quintuple selecter has 5 values that together are unique in selecting a database item.
        A database might express the <WWWWH> as [W, W, W, W, H] which implies a 5-dimension array.

    A release note detailing modifications limits what it communicates, to facts about the release:
        <who> <what> refers to some person or organization making changes to the managed object;
        <when> is by definition now or the milestone the activity may be associated with;
        <where> and <how> are answered in the project plan, design and architecture documents;
        <why> is like the effect of the moon on the tides, you can't resolve that here.

